---
registryId: riak
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Riak?

**Riak KV is a distributed key/value database designed to keep individual
object reads and writes available while machines or network paths fail.** A
client addresses one opaque value by bucket type, bucket, and key. Any cluster
node can coordinate the request, find the object's replica positions, and wait
for the acknowledgement policy chosen by the application.

Riak matters when a workload needs:

- direct key lookups with no permanent primary node;
- replication across several failure domains;
- online node addition, removal, and replacement;
- explicit control over read and write acknowledgement thresholds;
- application-owned conflict handling for concurrent updates.

The availability is not free. A successful response can leave temporary
fallback copies, divergent replicas, or sibling values that must later
converge.

{% callout variant="info" %}
### Core invariant

**Riak stays available by separating request success from final convergence.**
`N`, `R`, and `W` decide when a client may receive success. Causal context,
sibling resolution, hinted handoff, read repair, and active anti-entropy decide
whether every replica eventually stores an acceptable descendant.
{% /callout %}

This lesson focuses on Riak's Dynamo-style, eventually consistent key/value
behavior. Riak KV 2.x also supports strongly consistent bucket types, which use
a different coordination and availability path.

Read [database fundamentals](/fundamentals/database-fundamentals) first if
keys, replication, or consistency models are new. Review
[consistent hashing](/technology/consistent-hashing) for the broader placement
technique that Riak applies with a ring and virtual nodes.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Model one access path as one key

Riak's data model is a hierarchy that attaches policy to a direct lookup:

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Bucket type" eyebrow="Shared policy" icon="settings" tone="blue" %}
A bucket type groups properties such as `n_val`, quorum defaults, sibling
behavior, and data type. Create and activate it deliberately before using it.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Bucket" eyebrow="Namespace" icon="folder" tone="cyan" %}
A bucket groups keys inside a type. It is closer to a named keyspace than a
relational table with columns, joins, and constraints.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Key" eyebrow="Direct address" icon="key" tone="violet" %}
The key identifies one object inside the bucket. The bucket-and-key location is
hashed to find its ring partition and preference list.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Object" eyebrow="Value plus context" icon="database" tone="amber" %}
Riak stores opaque bytes with metadata and causal context. The application
owns the value schema, validation, and merge meaning.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% system-flow %}
{% system-node title="Business request" eyebrow="Known identifier" icon="send" tone="blue" %}
The caller already knows a stable identifier such as `cart-1842` or
`customer-73`.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Bucket policy" eyebrow="Type and bucket" icon="settings" tone="cyan" %}
The selected bucket type supplies replication, quorum, and sibling behavior.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="One key lookup" eyebrow="Primary access path" icon="key" tone="violet" %}
Riak hashes the location and routes directly to the object's replica
preference list.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Opaque value" eyebrow="Application schema" icon="file" tone="amber" %}
The client decodes, validates, and, when necessary, resolves the returned
object versions.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Strong production fits

- **Shopping cart:** `carts/active/cart-1842`, with an add-only item merge and
  an explicit rule for single-choice fields such as delivery method.
- **Customer preferences:** `profiles/customers/customer-73`, with a schema
  version and named authority for billing-owned versus user-owned fields.
- **Device snapshot:** `devices/latest/device-991`, with a monotonic device
  sequence that rejects older telemetry retries.

### Reconsider the model

- The main request needs joins, range scans, or ad hoc predicates instead of a
  known key.
- Several keys must change atomically as one business transaction.
- One unavoidable hot key receives more traffic than a single replica set can
  absorb.
- Values are large blobs whose transfer, repair, and sibling amplification
  dominate request latency.

The [Riak key/value modeling guide](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/developing/key-value-modeling/)
describes the type, bucket, and key address. Treat secondary indexes and search
as separate access paths with their own failure and coverage behavior, not as a
replacement for a primary-key model.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Follow a key from hash to vnode

Riak maps the hash space onto a fixed ring of partitions. A **virtual node
(vnode)** is the process responsible for one partition. Each physical Riak
node runs many vnodes, so ownership and transfer work can be spread more evenly
than a one-token-per-machine design.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Hash the location" label="Bucket and key" %}
Hash the object's bucket-and-key location into the ring's ordered key space.
The same location maps to the same initial partition while the ring definition
is unchanged.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Find the partition" label="Ring metadata" %}
Locate the partition that owns the hash position. Its vnode performs storage
work for that part of the key space.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Build the preference list" label="Replica placement" %}
Walk later ring positions to identify the primary replica positions required
by the bucket's `N` value.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Coordinate the request" label="Any Riak node" %}
The contacted node routes work to the preference list and waits for the
selected read or write acknowledgement condition.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

Vnodes provide movement units as well as storage units:

- Adding or removing a physical node reassigns a subset of partitions rather
  than every key.
- A node owns several separated partitions, which spreads its keys and lets
  several peers participate in transfers.
- A vnode is not an extra durable machine. Several vnodes can share one host,
  disk, power supply, and network failure.
- The ring size is chosen when the cluster is created. It affects balancing
  granularity, process overhead, and future cluster growth.

The [vnode guide](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/learn/concepts/vnodes/)
and [cluster guide](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/learn/concepts/clusters/)
describe the ring, partition ownership, and request coordination.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Separate replica count from acknowledgement count

Riak exposes three central values for eventually consistent objects:

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="N" eyebrow="Replica positions" icon="copy" tone="blue" %}
`N`, stored as `n_val`, is the number of primary ring positions responsible
for each object. Raising it increases copy, storage, network, repair, and
handoff work.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="R" eyebrow="Read responses" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
`R` is the number of replica responses required before a read can succeed.
Lower values can answer through more failures but can provide less overlap with
the latest acknowledged write.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="W" eyebrow="Write acknowledgements" icon="check" tone="violet" %}
`W` is the number of receipt acknowledgements required before a write can
succeed. `DW` can separately require durable-storage acknowledgements.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

For completed operations against the same logical `N` replica set:

`R + W > N`

means every successful read response set and successful write
acknowledgement set must overlap at least one position. With `N=3`, `R=2`,
and `W=2`, the two sets overlap.

That arithmetic is useful but narrower than "strong consistency":

- concurrent writers can create siblings because neither version causally
  descends from the other;
- sloppy quorum can involve fallback vnodes outside the original primary
  positions;
- a timeout is ambiguous because some replicas may already hold the write;
- overlap does not create multi-key transactions or serialize all clients.

`PR` and `PW` add primary-vnode requirements. They can reduce the chance that a
request succeeds entirely through fallback positions, but they also reduce
availability when primary vnodes are unreachable.

### How sloppy quorum creates recovery work

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Try primary positions" label="Preference list" %}
The coordinator contacts the first `N` vnode positions responsible for the
object.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Walk to fallbacks" label="Sloppy quorum" %}
If a primary is unavailable and policy permits, later reachable vnodes can
count toward `R` or `W`.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Return by threshold" label="Client contract" %}
The coordinator returns when the selected acknowledgement conditions are met,
even though a temporary placement may remain.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Hand data back" label="Hinted handoff" %}
When the primary owner returns, the fallback vnode transfers its temporary
responsibility and operators verify that handoff drains.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

The [replication guide](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/developing/usage/replication/)
documents `N`, `R`, `W`, `PR`, `PW`, and sloppy quorum. Choose them per
request contract instead of treating one cluster-wide tuple as universally
correct.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Lab 1: tune quorum behavior under replica loss

Use the workbench to change two independent properties:

1. Set how many copies exist and how many responses a read or write requires.
2. Decide whether fallback vnodes may satisfy the request when primaries are
   unavailable.

Watch client availability, response-set overlap, temporary placements, and
handoff debt change together. The model counts reachable vnode positions; it
does not estimate real network latency or prove that every physical failure
domain is independent.

{% interactive-block id="technology/riak-performance" dataFile="/api/content/technology/riak/data/quorum-envelope.json" /%}

Use the result to write a concrete contract:

- which completed write must be visible to which later read;
- how many primary failures the operation must tolerate;
- whether temporary fallback placement is acceptable;
- what makes a timeout safe to retry;
- which handoff evidence closes the incident.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Preserve concurrency with causal context

Causal context records whether one object version descends from another.
Riak versions use vector clocks or, with suitable bucket types, dotted version
vectors. The metadata helps Riak distinguish a causal update from concurrent
branches; it does not decide which business value is correct.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Read value and context" label="Observe" %}
The client fetches the object, validates every returned sibling, and retains
the opaque causal context from the response.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Apply one domain change" label="Modify" %}
Change the value according to its schema and authority rules. Avoid rebuilding
the object from an old cache without the observed context.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Write with context" label="Descend" %}
Send the updated value with the returned context so Riak can recognize it as a
causal descendant of what the client observed.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Resolve siblings explicitly" label="Converge" %}
If the read returns concurrent siblings, merge or choose by a documented
business rule, then write the result with the combined response context.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

With sibling creation enabled, two clients that write from the same base
context can produce two stored values for one key. This is safer than silently
discarding a concurrent change, but the application must bound and observe the
resolution path.

Avoid using wall-clock "last write wins" as a universal resolver:

- clocks can be skewed;
- arrival order does not encode user intent;
- selecting one whole object can drop independent fields from another sibling;
- a lossy choice can converge perfectly and still be wrong.

The [causal context guide](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/learn/concepts/causal-context/)
explains vector clocks, dotted version vectors, and siblings. Riak's data types
can provide built-in convergent semantics for supported structures, but an
ordinary opaque object still needs an application rule.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Lab 2: resolve meaning before repairing replicas

Choose an update history, a resolution policy, and a convergence action.
Observe three separate outcomes:

- whether the policy preserves valid concurrent changes;
- whether it can produce one defensible value;
- whether the application writes a causal descendant or leaves siblings for
  the next reader.

{% interactive-block id="technology/riak-convergence-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/riak/data/conflict-convergence-model.json" /%}

The key distinction is operational: **repair synchronizes stored versions;
application resolution decides their meaning.** Waiting for read repair or
active anti-entropy cannot turn two valid delivery choices into one correct
customer decision.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Implement a causal read-modify-write boundary

A production client should keep quorum policy, causal context, schema
validation, and conflict handling visible in code.

The HTTP example reads `X-Riak-Vclock`, stops if context is missing, and sends
that context with the update. Its `R`, `PR`, `W`, `PW`, and `DW` values are an
example request contract, not a universal recommendation.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/riak/code/causal-http-update.sh" language="bash" title="Causal HTTP update with explicit quorum policy" /%}

The merge example uses an add-only union for cart item identifiers but refuses
to invent a winner when concurrent siblings disagree on delivery method. The
caller must store the resolved value with the response context after the
function returns.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/riak/code/merge-cart-siblings.py" language="python" title="Domain merge for cart siblings" /%}

Production code also needs:

- bounded deadlines and idempotent retry identifiers;
- schema and size validation for every sibling;
- a maximum sibling count that triggers isolation or review;
- metrics for resolution rate, failures, lost-change prevention, and write-back;
- a dead-letter or operator workflow for conflicts without an automatic rule.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Use three different convergence mechanisms

Replica recovery is not one generic "repair" operation:

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Hinted handoff" eyebrow="Temporary ownership" icon="repeat" tone="amber" %}
A fallback vnode temporarily accepts responsibility for an unavailable primary
position, then transfers that responsibility back when the owner returns. It
addresses short-lived placement failure.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Read repair" eyebrow="Access-triggered repair" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
A read can discover missing or divergent stored versions among replicas and
repair what it observes. Cold keys may not be read often enough for this path
to cover the full data set.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Active anti-entropy" eyebrow="Background comparison" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
AAE exchanges Merkle-tree hashes between replicas, narrows differences to
specific objects, and repairs missing or divergent copies without waiting for
application traffic.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Recover a failed node as a measured workflow

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Identify the actual boundary" label="Detect" %}
Confirm which physical nodes, vnodes, primary positions, disks, and network
paths are unavailable. A process heartbeat alone is not enough.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Protect foreground traffic" label="Contain" %}
Use the documented quorum and retry contract. Reduce nonessential load so
handoff, repair, and client requests do not exhaust the same disk or network.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Return or replace ownership" label="Recover" %}
Bring the original node back only when its storage is trustworthy, or follow
the planned cluster-change and partition-repair procedure for a replacement.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Prove convergence" label="Verify" %}
Check ring ownership, pending handoff, AAE status, sibling trends, read/write
latency, errors, and disk headroom before closing the incident.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

The [handoff reference](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/using/reference/handoff/)
separates temporary hinted handoff from permanent ownership transfer. The
[AAE guide](https://docs.riak.com/riak/kv/latest/learn/concepts/active-anti-entropy/)
explains background hash-tree exchange.

Do not treat replication as backup. A valid but destructive application write
can replicate and repair successfully. Keep tested backups or an independent
recovery source for corruption, operator error, and application mistakes.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Plan capacity for copies and maintenance

Capacity starts with live logical values, not request rate alone. For a simple
baseline:

`logical bytes = live keys x average stored object bytes`

`replica bytes = logical bytes x N`

An example with 200 million live objects averaging 2 KB and `N=3` gives:

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="400 GB" label="Logical values" detail="200M x 2 KB in decimal units" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="1.2 TB" label="Replica baseline" detail="400 GB x N=3" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="1.72 TB+" label="At a 70% ceiling" detail="Example policy before other overhead" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

The 70% ceiling is an example operator policy, not a Riak constant. Add measured
allowances for metadata, indexes, storage-engine amplification, AAE trees,
temporary fallback copies, handoff, ownership transfer, backup staging, and
growth during the recovery window.

### Capacity questions

- **Object shape**
  - What are p50, p95, p99, and maximum serialized object sizes?
  - How many siblings can one hot key accumulate before reads become unsafe?
- **Distribution**
  - Are vnode ownership, bytes, operations, and latency balanced by physical
    node?
  - Does one key or bucket dominate a replica set even when the ring is balanced?
- **Maintenance**
  - Can handoff and AAE run beside peak traffic without exhausting disk I/O,
    network, or vnode mailboxes?
  - How long does adding, replacing, or restoring a node take at current data
    volume?
- **Failure headroom**
  - Can the remaining nodes accept foreground traffic plus temporary copies
    after the largest planned failure?
  - At what disk or latency threshold must operators stop topology changes?

Monitor request latency percentiles and errors with ring ownership, handoff
queues, AAE exchanges, object size, sibling counts, backend disk behavior,
memory, open files, and network saturation. A healthy average can hide one
overloaded vnode or replica set.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Choose Riak for the trade-off it actually makes

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Riak is a strong fit" eyebrow="Available key/value service" icon="check" tone="green" %}
- Requests address one bounded object by a known key.
- Serving through replica and network failure is more important than immediate
  global agreement.
- The domain has deterministic merge rules or can use supported convergent
  data types.
- The team can operate ring changes, handoff, AAE, backup, and restore.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Use another primary store" eyebrow="Different contract" icon="x" tone="rose" %}
- The workload depends on relational joins, broad scans, or ad hoc analytics.
- Multi-key serializable transactions are the central correctness boundary.
- Concurrent values cannot be reconciled or safely escalated.
- The team cannot reserve and monitor capacity for continuous convergence work.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Production review

Before launch, require evidence for each area:

1. **Data contract:** every object has a schema version, size bound, ownership
   rule, and primary-key access path.
2. **Replica contract:** `N`, `R`, `W`, `PR`, `PW`, and `DW` are documented per
   important operation, including timeout and retry semantics.
3. **Conflict contract:** concurrent and stale-context writes have automated
   tests, bounded sibling behavior, write-back, and escalation.
4. **Topology contract:** physical nodes, vnodes, racks, power, network, and
   storage volumes represent the failure domains assumed by the quorum model.
5. **Recovery contract:** handoff, AAE, node replacement, backup restore, and
   force-removal decisions are rehearsed with production-shaped data.
6. **Capacity contract:** foreground load and the largest maintenance flow fit
   together under the agreed disk, network, latency, and time ceilings.

Riak is not "set and forget." Its masterless request path removes a permanent
leader, but it makes placement, causal resolution, background repair, and
failure headroom first-class application and operations responsibilities.
{% /section-card %}

